```ADD_PAPER
2201.12150v2
A survey of learning curves for decision making in supervised machine learning. The paper formalizes sample-wise and iteration-wise learning curves and presents a unified framework categorizing learning-curve methods along three axes: the decision-making situation (data acquisition, early stopping, early discarding for model selection), the technical question addressed (binary comparisons, ranking, saturation values/points, bounds, full-curve models, and portfolio-level models), and the data resources used (target-dataset curves, other-dataset curves, dataset meta-features, learner features). The survey reviews a broad literature, including inverse power-law models, MCMC-based extrapolation, Gaussian-process surrogate models, and metalearning approaches, and discusses how these methods can predict the value of additional training data. This is directly relevant to data-efficiency experiments that compare models trained on 25% versus 100% of a training pool, since learning-curve extrapolation and saturation analysis provide the theoretical basis for quantifying how much performance can be gained from more data.
``` 
