```ADD_PAPER
2102.09382v1
This paper empirically studies how much training data is sufficient for classification. Using 20 UCI benchmark datasets and five classifiers (logistic regression, naïve Bayes, neural networks, random forests, and SVM where applicable), the authors systematically withheld training data at many sizes, computed 80% confidence intervals for best achievable accuracy at each size, and fit inverse power law learning curves y = α - βx^γ to the interval lower bounds. They defined a sufficient training set size (STSS) as the minimum training size whose projected accuracy is within 5% of the estimated asymptotic accuracy with 90% confidence. Estimated STSS values varied widely across datasets (e.g., 50 for several quickly saturating sets, 677–2,044 for moderate sets, and 7,598–18,944 for the hardest multi-class sets). Stepwise linear regression on dataset characteristics showed that STSS increases with the number of classes (p<0.001) and decreases with the number of features (p=0.038), leading to recommendations of roughly 3,000 training points for two-class problems and 10,000–30,000 points for multi-class problems depending on feature count. The paper also reports that feature selection improved accuracy by 4.5% on average on a subset of datasets, while neural-network parameter tuning did not help because it consumed training data. This is directly relevant to the controlled data-efficiency experiment: it provides the learning-curve/saturation framework and empirical evidence for how much accuracy can be gained (or not) when moving from a small fraction (e.g., 25%) of a training pool to the full pool, and it warns that the gain depends strongly on dataset class count, feature count, and the learner used.
``` 
