Reviews: Reviewer #1:
(2.8636363636363638, 'The performance of your submission is: 2.8636363636363638THOUGHT:\nThis submission is a write-up of a "controlled" data-efficiency experiment that never actually reports the experimental outcomes. The core measurements—accuracy at 25% data, accuracy at 100% data, the efficiency ratio, and the learning-curve fit—are all missing because the authors intentionally printed verbose training logs before the final results, and the log was truncated by an external evidence window. The paper then presents this self-inflicted truncation as a "methodological finding." That is not a substantive contribution for a top ML venue. The experimental design is clearly described and the writing is organized, but the paper lacks any actual results and its central research question is unanswered. I would reject this submission: it is more like a debugging note or failed-run report than a complete research paper. The soundness is poor because the central claims are not supported by evidence; the contribution is minimal; and the methodological "lesson" is essentially "print final metrics early," which is obvious and easily fixed.\n\nI will assign low ratings across originality, quality, significance, and overall, and set the decision to Reject. Confidence is reasonably high because the issues are clear from the text itself.REVIEW JSON:\n```json\n{\n  "Summary": "The paper describes a controlled data-efficiency experiment comparing multinomial logistic regression trained on 25% of a 400-sample synthetic training pool (100 samples) versus the full 400-sample pool. The authors plan to report held-out test accuracy for both arms and an efficiency ratio, and additionally fit an inverse power-law learning curve over multiple training-pool prefix sizes. However, the submitted paper reports that the final measurements were not captured because the training script emitted verbose per-step loss logs before printing the summary metrics, exceeding the 1,000-character evidence-window boundary. The paper therefore contains no efficiency ratio, no final accuracies, no learning-curve fit, and no completed empirical analysis. Its main claimed contribution is a methodological observation that output truncation can hide decisive results in automated evaluation pipelines.",\n  "Strengths": [\n    "The proposed experimental design is well-specified and reproducible: fixed seed, synthetic data, fixed class centers, fixed split, and identical model/training settings across arms.",\n    "The authors are honest that the decisive measurements were not captured; they do not fabricate results.",\n    "The inverse power-law fitting procedure is clearly described, even though it is never actually applied to reported data.",\n    "The paper highlights a real practical issue in automated ML evaluation: verbose logging before result emission can cause final metrics to be lost under output-capture limits."\n  ],\n  "Weaknesses": [\n    "The central empirical question is completely unanswered: no values for eff.acc_at_25, eff.acc_at_100, or eff.efficiency_ratio are reported.",\n    "The learning-curve saturation analysis is described extensively but never executed with reported data; no fitted parameters, RMSE, or sufficient training-set size are given.",\n    "The claimed \'methodological finding\' is self-inflicted and trivial: printing verbose logs before final summaries causes truncation. This is a debugging issue, not a research contribution.",\n    "The paper contains no verifiable experimental artifacts, such as full captured stdout, recorded metric outputs, or code listing.",\n    "Related work is mentioned only by author names (e.g., Mohr and van Rijn, Rosenfeld et al., Domhan et al.) without a proper bibliography or citations.",\n    "The paper is not a complete research study: it lacks a real results section and a conclusion, and large portions of the text are repetitive."\n  ],\n  "Originality": 1,\n  "Quality": 1,\n  "Clarity": 2,\n  "Significance": 1,\n  "Questions": [\n    "Can the authors provide the actual values of eff.acc_at_25, eff.acc_at_100, eff.efficiency_ratio, and eff.train_s from a rerun with corrected logging order, or from the structured record_result output? Without them, the paper\'s main question remains unanswered.",\n    "What exactly is the \'legacy evidence-window boundary\'? Was it imposed by a specific evaluation harness, and why was the experiment not simply rerun with output order fixed before submission?",\n    "How is the output-truncation \'finding\' more than a trivial bug report? What generalizable design principle beyond \'print final results first\' is being proposed?",\n    "What were the nine prefix accuracies and the fitted power-law parameters? If the code computed them, why were they not written to a separate structured log before verbose training output?",\n    "Can the authors include the exact code and the full captured stdout trace, and confirm whether any record_result calls were actually executed successfully?"\n  ],\n  "Limitations": [\n    "No primary empirical measurements are reported, so the data-efficiency question is not answered.",\n    "The experimental setup uses only one synthetic dataset and one linear model, limiting any potential empirical generalization even if the results were recovered.",\n    "The paper provides no artifact or code, despite claiming reproducibility from the text alone.",\n    "The evidence-window size and truncation behavior are not formally defined or analyzed; the \'finding\' is anecdotal.",\n    "The work does not compare against any existing method or baseline and does not advance the state of the art in learning-curve analysis, active learning, or evaluation methodology."\n  ],\n  "Ethical Concerns": false,\n  "Soundness": 1,\n  "Presentation": 2,\n  "Contribution": 1,\n  "Overall": 2,\n  "Confidence": 4,\n  "Decision": "Reject"\n}\n```', True), 
Reviewer #2:
(3.886363636363635, 'The performance of your submission is: 3.886363636363635THOUGHT:\nThe paper is a well-written but ultimately empty empirical study. It describes a controlled synthetic data-efficiency experiment for multinomial logistic regression, but the final objective measurements were lost due to stdout truncation. The paper therefore reports no efficiency ratio, no accuracies, and no learning-curve fit. The only claimed contribution is a methodological caution about verbose logging and evidence windows, but this is a banal observation about output truncation rather than a substantive research contribution. The paper is transparent about its failure and includes relevant background, but it is not a complete piece of work and has essentially no empirical content. I would reject it: the research question is unanswered, the methodological "finding" is not novel, and the write-up, while clear, is padded with extraneous material.\n\nREVIEW JSON:\n```json\n{\n  "Summary": "The paper aims to quantify the data efficiency of multinomial logistic regression by comparing test accuracy after training on 100 samples (25% of a 400-sample training pool) versus 400 samples, using a fully synthetic dataset generated with a fixed seed. It defines an efficiency ratio, describes a controlled protocol, and also proposes an inverse power-law learning-curve analysis. However, the reported execution produced more than 1,000 characters of per-step training logs before final measurements, causing the captured evidence to be truncated. As a result, no accuracy values, efficiency ratio, training time, or learning-curve fit are actually reported. The paper\'s main conclusion is a methodological caution about output truncation in automated evaluation pipelines.",\n  "Strengths": [\n    "The experimental design is clearly described and the deterministic setup (seed, synthetic data, fixed model, fixed optimizer) would make a completed run easy to reproduce.",\n    "The paper is transparent about the missing results and does not fabricate accuracy numbers.",\n    "The writing is organized and covers background material on learning curves, related work, and limitations in a readable way."\n  ],\n  "Weaknesses": [\n    "The central research question is not answered: the efficiency ratio, test accuracies, training time, and learning-curve parameters are all absent from the reported evidence.",\n    "The claimed contribution is a methodological observation about verbose output causing truncation, which is a trivial and well-known failure mode rather than a novel scientific finding.",\n    "No actual code, raw log, or structured record of the computed metrics is included, so even the claim that the results were computed but truncated cannot be fully verified.",\n    "The paper includes substantial background and discussion sections but no meaningful experimental results, making it an incomplete study rather than a finished research paper.",\n    "The text refers to figures being included, but the document contains no figures; this further weakens the reproducibility and completeness of the presentation.",\n    "Even if the experiment had completed, a single synthetic dataset, one seed, and one linear model would provide only a very limited basis for generalizable data-efficiency conclusions."\n  ],\n  "Originality": 1,\n  "Quality": 2,\n  "Clarity": 3,\n  "Significance": 1,\n  "Questions": [\n    "Can the authors provide the raw truncated stdout or a minimal code snippet that demonstrates the claimed truncation before the final measurement lines?",\n    "Was any structured record of the metrics written outside stdout? If so, what were the actual values of acc_at_25, acc_at_100, efficiency_ratio, and train_s?",\n    "Can the experiment be rerun with the logging reordered or reduced so that the final results are captured? If so, what are the resulting data-efficiency metrics?",\n    "Why should the output-truncation observation be considered a generalizable methodological contribution rather than a specific artifact of the particular evaluation harness?",\n    "Where are the figures mentioned in the Discussion? Their absence contradicts the claim that visualization outputs were produced."\n  ],\n  "Limitations": [\n    "The primary limitation is the complete absence of measured results: the headline metric and all supporting measurements are missing.",\n    "The study is limited to one synthetic dataset, one model class, one seed, and one fixed training budget, so even a completed run would have limited generalizability.",\n    "The output-truncation issue depends on the specific evidence-window behavior of the evaluation platform and may not generalize to other settings.",\n    "No negative societal impacts are identified, which is reasonable for a synthetic-data methodological study."\n  ],\n  "Ethical Concerns": false,\n  "Soundness": 2,\n  "Presentation": 3,\n  "Contribution": 1,\n  "Overall": 3,\n  "Confidence": 4,\n  "Decision": "Reject"\n}\n```', True), 
Reviewer #3:
(4.545454545454545, 'The performance of your submission is: 4.545454545454545THOUGHT:\nThis is a peculiar submission: it is a technical report of a controlled experiment where the decisive measurements (test accuracies) were never captured in the evidence window because the stdout exceeded 1000 characters. The paper is clearly written and the experimental protocol is clearly specified, but the central empirical question (efficiency ratio) is not answered. The only real "result" is a methodological caution about output truncation in automated pipelines. That caution is not novel enough for a prestigious ML venue, and the paper over-states the significance of a null/logging artifact. The paper would be more appropriate as a "lessons learned" blog post or appendix note. The lack of any accuracy numbers, efficiency ratio, or learning curve fit means the main scientific contribution is absent. The experimental design is sound in principle, and the authors are honest about the failure, but the contribution is largely negative and limited. I recommend reject.\n\nNeed to fill JSON with ratings: Originality low (2 or 1?), Quality fair (2), Clarity good (3), Significance low (1 or 2). Overall 3 or 4. Since it\'s technically solid but no results, borderline reject. However, the requested Decision only Accept/Reject, so Reject. Soundness: fair (2). Presentation: good (3). Contribution: poor (1) or fair (2). Let\'s set contribution to 1 because the paper provides no usable empirical result and its methodological point is common knowledge. Overall: 4 (borderline reject) but as reject. Need maybe use "4" for borderline reject. But "Decision" must be Reject. Let\'s craft concise but detailed JSON.REVIEW JSON:\n```json\n{\n  "Summary": "The paper reports a controlled data-efficiency experiment for multinomial logistic regression on a synthetic classification dataset. The plan compares training on 100 samples (25% of a 400-sample pool) against training on all 400 samples, with identical model, optimizer, initialization, and evaluation. It also proposes a learning-curve saturation analysis using an inverse power-law fit. The paper reports that because the training loop prints more than 1000 characters of per-step loss logs before the final measurements are printed, the legacy evidence window was truncated, and the decisive accuracy values and efficiency ratio were not captured. The main claimed contribution is a methodological warning about output truncation in automated evaluation pipelines.",\n  "Strengths": [\n    "The experimental design is carefully controlled and fully specified: synthetic data, fixed seed, fixed train/test split, identical hyperparameters across arms.",\n    "The paper is clearly written and transparently acknowledges that the decisive metrics were not recovered.",\n    "The optimization sanity checks (initial cross-entropy equals ln 3, monotone decrease) confirm that the implemented training pipeline is correct up to the truncation point.",\n    "The reproducibility details are thorough, including the data-generation formula, gradient equations, and hyperparameter table."\n  ],\n  "Weaknesses": [\n    "The central empirical question is unanswered: no test accuracies, efficiency ratio, or learning-curve fits are reported.",\n    "The paper draws a strong methodological conclusion from an artificial and self-inflicted logging failure, which is not a novel or broadly informative contribution to the machine learning community.",\n    "The experiment could be re-run trivially by moving the record_result calls before the verbose logging or printing a compact summary; the authors explicitly note this, which further weakens the significance of the reported failure.",\n    "The learning-curve saturation analysis is specified but not executed/validated; the fitted parameters and STSS are absent.",\n    "The paper over-claims by presenting an execution-and-truncation artifact as a principal ‘positive result’ for methodology, while the actual data-efficiency findings are entirely missing.",\n    "Some sections (Background, Related Work) read as filler and are only loosely connected to the null empirical outcome."\n  ],\n  "Originality": 2,\n  "Quality": 2,\n  "Clarity": 3,\n  "Significance": 1,\n  "Questions": [\n    "Could the authors simply re-run the experiment with record_result emitted immediately after each training arm, before verbose logs, and provide the missing accuracy values, efficiency ratio, and learning-curve fit?",\n    "What is the actual measured efficiency ratio? The paper gives no value at all, so the reader cannot assess even the direction of the effect.",\n    "Why was the log not redirected to a file or the verbosity reduced, given that the 1000-character limit was anticipated?",\n    "Is the truncation behavior reproducible with the submitted code and environment? Could the authors provide the recorded metrics from a local rerun?",\n    "The paper argues that output truncation is a substantive methodological finding. Are there existing best-practice guidelines in automated ML pipelines that already recommend structured logging or early emission of final metrics? How does this work advance beyond those?"\n  ],\n  "Limitations": [\n    "The paper contains no empirical results; the efficiency ratio and learning-curve analysis are entirely absent.",\n    "The methodological lesson is limited to a single, self-imposed logging failure and is not evaluated across different harnesses or pipeline settings.",\n    "The synthetic dataset and linear model limit generalizability, and the paper does not provide any completed baseline measurement.",\n    "Potential negative societal impact is minimal, but the lack of findings means there is also little positive utility for practitioners."\n  ],\n  "Ethical Concerns": false,\n  "Soundness": 2,\n  "Presentation": 3,\n  "Contribution": 1,\n  "Overall": 4,\n  "Confidence": 5,\n  "Decision": "Reject"\n}\n```', True)
